<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uruguay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uruguay is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. In the early nineteenth century the territory, then known as the Cisplatine Province, was contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, and the costly war over it ended with Uruguayan independence in 1828. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> also note that Uruguay contributed forces alongside Brazil and Argentina in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uruguay.jpg" alt="Uruguay" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uruguay.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Uruguay covers about 176,214 square kilometres of continental land and is the second-smallest sovereign nation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>. The landscape is mostly rolling plains and low hill ranges called cuchillas, with a fertile coastal lowland and 660 kilometres of coastline. The highest point is Cerro Catedral, at 514 metres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> lies to the south-west, and the Río Negro, dammed in 1945, formed the artificial Rincón del Bonete Lake. Montevideo is the southernmost national capital in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and Uruguay lies entirely south of the Tropic of Capricorn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hunter-gatherers lived in the region about 13,000 years ago. At the first contact with Europeans in the 16th century there were an estimated 9,000 Charrúa and 6,000 Chaná, with some Guaraní island settlements. Eastern Uruguay holds man-made tumuli called Cerritos de Indios, some about 5,000 years old. The Portuguese entered the region in 1512, and the Spanish in 1515. Fierce resistance and the lack of valuable resources limited settlement, the Spanish introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> in 1603, and the first permanent Spanish settlement was Soriano in 1624. The Portuguese built a fort at Colonia del Sacramento in 1680, and the Spanish founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1726 as a military stronghold.</p><p>British forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> from February to September 1807. In 1811 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a>, later Uruguay's national hero, led a revolt against Spanish authority and won the Battle of Las Piedras on 18 May. He became a champion of federalism and demanded autonomy for the Banda Oriental, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> pursued unitary centralism. Independence was secured between 1811 and 1828, after a struggle involving Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the United Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a>, and a constitution was enacted in 1830.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>From the second half of the 19th century migration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> reshaped the population, and in the 20th century Uruguay became an advanced welfare state nicknamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Tensions during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, including the activities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupamaros">Tupamaros</a> urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> movement, eroded democratic institutions, and a coup in 1973 began a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> that lasted until 1985.</p><p>Uruguay is now a constitutional republic with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a>. Its 1967 constitution allows citizens to propose bills and call referendums, a feature drawn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Montevideo's metropolitan area holds about 1.9 million of its almost 3.5 million people, and 86.0 per cent identified as white in the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uruguay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Paraguay under Francisco Solano López seized Corrientes in 1865, Argentina joined Brazil and <b>Uruguay</b> in the Treaty of the Triple Alliance, and Mitre commanded the allied armies in the early campaigns, including the costly defeat at the Battle of Curupayty in 1866.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His ambitions in the Río de la Plata produced the costly Cisplatine War (1825–1828), which ended with the independence of <b>Uruguay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 11 December 2013 <b>Uruguay</b> became the first country in the world to fully legalise the growth, use and sale of cannabis, by a Senate vote of 16 to 13, under President José Mujica, a former Tupamaro guerrilla who had spent almost fifteen years in prison.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After Brazil intervened in <b>Uruguay</b>, López seized a Brazilian steamer in November 1864 and invaded Mato Grosso and then Argentine Corrientes, and Brazil, Argentina and Uruguay joined against him in the Treaty of the Triple Alliance of 1 May 1865.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Republic of Paraguay · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…1816 that Manuel García had encouraged to end the &quot;federal disease&quot;, passed to Portugal in 1820 and emerged as <b>Uruguay</b> in 1828 after the Cisplatine War and British intervention, by a Treaty of Montevideo that asked no Oriental's opinion and left the borders undrawn, to Brazil's later gain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their traditional range lies in what is now Paraguay between the Paraná River and the lower Paraguay River, the Misiones Province of Argentina, southern Brazil once as far east as Rio de Janeiro, and parts of <b>Uruguay</b> and Bolivia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/guarani">Guaraní · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
